  "final_text": "All four steps are done, and the read-backs match.\n\n1. **Data view 'Kibana Sample Data Logs'** (`kibana_sample_data_logs`, id `90943e30-9a47-11e8-b64d-95841ca0b247`): the time field is `timestamp`. Notable fields: `response` (text) with `response.keyword` (aggregatable), `bytes`, `clientip`, `url`, `host`, `agent`, `geo.*`, plus `@timestamp` and `utc_time`, which are also date fields.\n2. **Dashboard 'S4-aaa42e9e Web logs'** (`ba348002-63bd-4b4f-b1a7-860eb712c6f1`): two panels on the data view.\n   - A line chart of count over `timestamp` (date histogram), titled \"Requests over time\".\n   - A pie chart of count grouped by `response.keyword`, initially titled \"Requests by response code\".\n3. **Alert rule 'S4-aaa42e9e 5xx errors'** (`ebe6311a-4ec7-4c2d-b3c0-265830ef5c12`): an `.es-query` rule, checked every 1m. It fires when the count is greater than 10 over a 5-minute window. The filter is `response.keyword >= \"500\" and response.keyword <= \"599\"`. The API created it enabled, so I didn't need to enable it.\n4. **Panel rename**: the pie panel is now titled \"Responses by code\". I read the dashboard back and it has exactly two panels, \"Requests over time\" and \"Responses by code\".\n\nChoices I made:\n- The 5xx filter uses a string range on `response.keyword`. That is exact for 3-digit codes, and the rule avoids ES|QL because I couldn't confirm the mapping through the API.\n- The line and pie panels use the classic data-view panels rather than ES|QL, so both are tied to `timestamp`.\n\nNot verified:\n- I couldn't query Elasticsearch directly (no ES endpoint is exposed), so I did not check the actual `response` values or the rule's match count against the data. The rule's execution status reads `ok`, which suggests the query is valid.\n- I didn't render the dashboard, so the panel visuals are unchecked.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
